Q. In the context of distributed systems, what is the main benefit of using a consistent hashing algorithm?

It minimizes the amount of data that needs to be redistributed when nodes are added or removed.


Q. When designing a system with high availability, which redundancy technique involves creating multiple instances of the same component to handle requests in parallel?

The redundancy technique you’re describing is called active-active redundancy.

Q. What is the primary purpose of using a message queue in a distributed system?

The primary purpose of using a message queue in a distributed system is to decouple components and enable asynchronous communication.

Q. What is a common technique for reducing the impact of the `N+1 query problem` in database-driven applications?

A common technique to reduce the impact of the N+1 query problem is eager loading (also called preloading).

Q. In a distributed system, how can you achieve eventual consistency?

In a distributed system, eventual consistency is achieved by ensuring that all replicas of a piece of data will converge to the same value over time, even if updates happen asynchronously.

Q. What is a common reason to implement sharding in a distributed database system?


A common reason to implement sharding in a distributed database system is to improve scalability by distributing data across multiple machines (shards) so that no single database node becomes a bottleneck.

Q. Which of the following is an example of vertical scaling?

👉 Upgrading a single server by adding more CPU, RAM, or faster storage so it can handle a larger workload.

Q. In a distributed system, which technique can be used to achieve fault tolerance?

In a distributed system, a common technique to achieve fault tolerance is replication.

Q. Which of the following is a common use case for implementing a publish-subscribe pattern in a distributed system?

👉 Event-driven communication, such as sending notifications or broadcasting updates to multiple subscribers.

Q. Which of the following is a key advantage of using a NoSQL database over a traditional relational database?

👉 Ability to scale horizontally and handle large volumes of unstructured or semi-structured data.

A company is developing a highly available web application using stateless web servers. Which TWO services are suitable for storing session state data?


Q. For a highly available web application with stateless web servers, the session state must be stored in a centralized, durable, and highly available service (not on the individual servers).

✅ Two suitable services are:

Distributed Cache (e.g., Redis, Memcached, AWS ElastiCache, Azure Cache for Redis)

Stores session state in memory for fast access.

Replication + clustering provide high availability.

Database (e.g., Amazon DynamoDB, Azure Cosmos DB, relational DB with HA setup)

Provides durable storage for session state.

Useful if sessions must survive cache evictions or restarts.

Q. Company salespeople upload their sales figures daily. A Solutions Architect needs a durable storage solution for these documents that also protects against users accidentally deleting important documents. Which action will protect against unintended user actions?


Enable storage versioning (e.g., S3 Versioning) to protect documents from accidental deletions or overwrites while maintaining durability.

Q. An application requires a highly available relational database with an initial storage capacity of 8 TB. The database will grow by 8 GB every day.To support expected traffic, at least eight read replicas will be required to handle database reads. Which option will meet these requirements?

Use Amazon Aurora (MySQL- or PostgreSQL-compatible) with Multi-AZ deployment. It supports the 8 TB+ initial size, auto-scales storage as the DB grows, and allows up to 15 read replicas for read scaling.

